# Hyperactivity: ten trillion dollars

Avik Jain, Hyperactive Labs, 2026-11-18

## The statement

Hyperactivity is the coordination layer of computation and credit. Every computation is identified by its content and carries its proof. Every work is credited through what it is built from and who carried it. Anything derived once is never paid for again. Credit is conserved, so it allocates what money allocated. The world spends $6.37 trillion a year on information technology (Gartner, July 2026), and the largest technology companies are worth $38.6 trillion together. Most of both is rent on recomputation, on intermediation and on location. Hyperactivity removes those rents, and the value they held moves into the layer that removed them. Ten trillion dollars is a quarter of it.

## The theorem the money follows

A computation run n times costs n·c when every run starts from nothing. That is how a language model sells inference: each answer is derived again and sold by the token, with no proof attached. In hyperactivity the same computation costs c once plus n lookups, because the result is stored under its content with its witness. The difference, (n − 1)·c, is the rent. As the verified graph grows, the share of all computation that is already derived goes to 1, and the price of anything already derived is 0.

The same holds for money. Money's value is 0 exactly where money is not the allocator of anything wanted (paper §10). Conserved credit (§10.6–10.8: conservation under allocate and deliver, with receipt sets) allocates information, trust and taste once they are unrationed. In abundance no power structure is needed to ration them.

## Where the ten trillion comes from

Three independent routes. Any one of them reaches the figure.

1. **Compute rent.** Of the $6.37 trillion a year, software ($1.47T), services ($1.57T) and data centre systems ($0.82T) are spent producing, integrating and re-running code. Under derive-once, code is written once, proven once and composed everywhere. Ten trillion dollars is under three years of the rent it removes.
2. **Intermediation.** Platforms, app stores, labels and ad networks earn by standing between people and the work. The wave (full credit through the reshare tree, no decay, credit equal to reachability) and the sample graph credit every contributor directly. The middle's share of the $38.6 trillion has nowhere to go but the graph.
3. **The allocator.** Gold is valued around $27.8 trillion and allocates nothing. Conserved credit allocates computation, creative work and trust across the whole graph. The network value of the allocator is bounded by what it allocates, which is the economy itself.

## The sequence of choices

The outcome is produced by decisions, not waited for. Eight fronts are already in motion:

- **Trading firms:** the whole firm becomes one program reasoning over its whole information space. The firm that adopts it out-earns the field, and every competitor follows.
- **Music:** Crowdsurf, the room and the wave, the stem player and Ye. Every record credited, talent revealed with no gatekeeper.
- **Medicine:** the chemistry knowledge graph for medicine research.
- **Education:** curriculum inversion for math fluency by childhood.
- **Language:** univalence with Unison; the lineage from Leibniz, Petri, McCarthy, Reynolds, Brock and Statebox brought into one room.
- **Intelligence:** symbolic reasoning over equality and higher paths, and post-training open models on the proven corpus.
- **The server:** the public hyperactive server, serving the work as language objects.
- **The people:** Musk, Naval, Choi, Rovelli, Kay, Berners-Lee, Minsky and Thiel, each written to on their own terms with full credit for what the work builds on.

Ye is the front with the widest reach to the people who adopt. People adopt what works correctly, works better than what they use and is free. They adopt when there is a frontier result worth having.

## Why the outcome is certain

Run sequences of choices in parallel. If sequence i reaches the outcome with probability pᵢ, the probability that none does is ∏(1 − pᵢ). A thousand sequences at 1% each fail together with probability 0.99¹⁰⁰⁰ ≈ 0.00004. At 0.5% each, 0.995¹⁰⁰⁰ ≈ 0.007. Agents execute the sequences at machine speed, so a thousand is the working number, not a ceiling.

A product is either running or not running. Its only shared dependency is the core, and the core is machine-checked mathematics, years of it, already done. So the sequences share no point of failure. They do share success: every result one sequence derives enters the graph, and every other sequence reuses it for free. Each success raises the probability of the rest.

## Precedent

Sixteen public companies are worth over $1 trillion, with a combined value above $35 trillion; in 2018 there was one. The heads of OpenAI and Anthropic both expect a billion-dollar company run by one person. One person directing agents is how hyperactivity was built. A trillion-dollar company is a tier with sixteen members. Ten trillion is the value of the layer beneath all of them.

